    attribution: Henri Frankfort, in the 1940s, on apparently contradictory Egyptian ideas
    section: content-and-meaning
timeline:
  - when: around 3100 BC
    what: Egypt is unified under the pharaohs at the end of the Predynastic
      Period, making the king the focus of Egyptian religion
    from: The unification of Egypt under the pharaohs, at the end of the
      Predynastic Period around 3100 BC, made the king the focus of Egyptian
      religion, and thus the ideology of kingship became an important part of
      mythology.
  - when: c. 2686–2181 BC
    what: In the Old Kingdom the most important mythological system is the cults
      of Ra and Atum at Heliopolis, whose Ennead was said to have created the
      world
    from: In the Old Kingdom (c. 2686–2181 BC) the most important of these systems
      was the cults of Ra and Atum, centered at Heliopolis. They formed a
      mythical family, the Ennead, that was said to have created the world.
  - when: c. 2686 BC – 2181 BC
    what: Many scholars have suggested that the myth of the sun god withdrawing
      into the sky was inspired by the breakdown of royal authority and
      national unity at the end of the Old Kingdom
    from: Many scholars have suggested that the myth of the sun god withdrawing
      into the sky, leaving humans to fight among themselves, was inspired by
      the breakdown of royal authority and national unity at the end of the
      Old Kingdom (c. 2686 BC – 2181 BC).
  - when: c. 1550–1070 BC
    what: In the New Kingdom, minor myths develop around deities like Yam and
      Anat, who had been adopted from Canaanite religion
    from: In the New Kingdom (c. 1550–1070 BC), minor myths developed around
      deities like Yam and Anat who had been adopted from Canaanite religion.
  - when: c. 1070–664 BC
    what: An inscription from the Third Intermediate Period, whose text may be
      much older, describes creation as an intellectual process and attributes
      it to Ptah
    from: An inscription from the Third Intermediate Period (c. 1070–664 BC),
      whose text may be much older, describes the process in detail and
      attributes it to the god Ptah, whose close association with craftsmen
      makes him a suitable deity to give a physical form to the original
      creative vision.
  - when: 332 BC–641 AD
    what: Through the Greek and Roman eras, Greco-Roman culture has little
      influence on Egyptian mythology
    from: In contrast, during the Greek and Roman eras (332 BC–641 AD),
      Greco-Roman culture had little influence on Egyptian mythology.
image-plan:
  - file: Nun_Raises_the_Sun.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "The moment of creation: Nun, the embodiment of the primordial
      waters, lifts the barque of the sun god Ra into the sky."
  - file: Nut1.JPG
    role: inline
    section: content-and-meaning
    caption: "The sky as a cow goddess held up by other deities. One image
      combining several coexisting visions of the sky: as a roof, as the
      surface of a sea, as a cow, and as a goddess in human form.[[ ^27 ]]"
  - file: Geb,_Nut,_Shu.jpg
    role: inline
    section: universe
    caption: The air god Shu, assisted by other gods, holds up Nut, the sky, while
      Geb, the earth, lies beneath.
  - file: Sunrise_at_Creation.jpg
    role: inline
    section: creation
    caption: The sun rises over the circular mound of creation, the first dry
      land, as goddesses pour out the primeval waters around it.
  - file: Flickr_-_Gaspa_-_Dendara,_tempio_di_Hator_(56).jpg
    role: inline
    section: osiris-myth
    caption: "Temple decoration at Dendera: the goddesses Isis and Nephthys watch
      over the corpse of their brother Osiris."
  - file: Hermitage_Egyptian_statuettes.jpg
    role: inline
    section: osiris-myth
    caption: Statues of Osiris and of Isis nursing the infant Horus, the heir she
      conceived after briefly reviving her husband.
  - file: Ra_Barque.jpg
    role: inline
    section: the-journey-of-the-sun
    caption: Ra on the solar barque, adorned with the sun-disk.
  - file: Book_of_Gates_Barque_of_Ra_cropped.jpg
    role: inline
    section: the-journey-of-the-sun
    caption: Ra (at center) travels through the sky in his barque, accompanied by
      other gods.[[ ^97 ]]
  - file: Set_speared_Apep.jpg
    role: inline
    section: the-journey-of-the-sun
    caption: The Mesektet Barque, with Ra as Set spearing Apophis in the
      underworld (Egyptian Museum, Cairo).
  - file: SethAndHorusAdoringRamsses_crop.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-religion
    caption: Set and Horus support the pharaoh. The reconciled rival gods often
      stand for the unity of Egypt under the rule of its king.[[ ^107 ]]
  - file: Hidden_treasures_19.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-art
    caption: A funerary amulet in the shape of a scarab. Scarab-shaped amulets
      symbolized the regeneration of life, referring to the god Khepri, the
      form the sun god was said to take at dawn.
glossary:
  - term: maat
    gloss: The fundamental order of the universe in Egyptian belief, established
      at the creation of the world and marking it off from the chaos that
      preceded and surrounds it.
    from: The Egyptian word written m3ˁt, often rendered maat or ma'at, refers to
      the fundamental order of the universe in Egyptian belief. Established at
      the creation of the world, maat distinguishes the world from the chaos
      that preceded and surrounds it.
  - term: Nun
    gloss: The god who personifies the infinite and chaotic primordial ocean that
      preceded the creation of the ordered world.
    from: In Egyptian belief, there already existed an infinite and chaotic
      primordial ocean that preceded the creation of the ordered world. This
      cosmic ocean was personified by the god Nun.
  - term: Duat
    gloss: A mysterious region bordering the formlessness of Nun, which Ra passes
      into beyond the western horizon at night and emerges from at dawn.
    from: At night Ra passes beyond the western horizon into the Duat, a
      mysterious region that borders the formlessness of Nun. At dawn he
      emerges from the Duat in the eastern horizon.
  - term: akhet
    gloss: The horizon, somewhere beyond the deserts, where two mountains in the
      east and the west mark the places the sun enters and exits the Duat.
    from: Somewhere beyond them is the horizon, the akhet. There, two mountains,
      in the east and the west, mark the places where the sun enters and exits
      the Duat.
  - term: Ennead
    gloss: A set of nine deities produced by Atum at the time of creation,
      including Geb, Nut, and other key elements of the world.
    from: At the time of creation he emerges to produce other gods, resulting in a
      set of nine deities, the Ennead, which includes Geb, Nut, and other key
      elements of the world.
  - term: heka
    gloss: "Magic: the force linking things in the divine realm to things in the
      physical world, and so the creator's original concept to its physical
      realization."
    from: The force of heka, or magic, which links things in the divine realm and
      things in the physical world, is the power that links the creator's
      original concept with its physical realization.
  - term: ba
    gloss: The soul of a deceased human, which may travel during the day but must
      return to its body each night.
    from: Ra, endlessly moving, is like the ba, or soul, of a deceased human,
      which may travel during the day but must return to its body each night.
  - term: historiola
    gloss: One of the elaborate new stories that, David Frankfurter argues,
      private rituals created by adapting basic mythic traditions to fit a
      specific ritual.
    from: The Egyptologist David Frankfurter argues that these rituals adapt basic
      mythic traditions to fit the specific ritual, creating elaborate new
      stories (called historiolas) based on myth.
further:
  - slug: ancient-egyptian-creation-myths
    why: The creation myths, in which the gods form the universe out of primordial
      chaos, are among the most important episodes from the mythic past.
  - slug: osiris
    why: The collection of episodes surrounding Osiris' death and succession is
      the most elaborate of all Egyptian myths, and had the most widespread
      influence in Egyptian culture.
  - slug: duat
    why: Ra's daily journey through the world has an otherworldly counterpart in
      the Duat, whose nature and location Egyptologists still disagree about.
anchors:
  hook: Few complete stories appear in Egyptian mythological sources. These
    sources often contain nothing more than allusions to the events to which
    they relate, and texts that contain actual narratives tell only portions
    of a larger story. Thus, for any given myth the Egyptians may have had
    only the general outlines of a story, from which fragments describing
    particular incidents were drawn.
  standfirst: Egyptian mythology is the collection of myths from ancient Egypt,
    which describe the actions of the Egyptian gods as a means of
    understanding the world around them. The beliefs that these myths express
    are an important part of ancient Egyptian religion. Myths appear
    frequently in Egyptian writings and art, particularly in short stories and
    in religious material such as hymns, ritual texts, funerary texts, and
    temple decoration. These sources rarely contain a complete account of a
    myth and often describe only brief fragments.
